WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.729 --> 00:00:07.210
Monday we did an analysis of that of value
added tax and one of the things I mentioned

00:00:07.210 --> 00:00:12.539
during that is that that taxes are effectively
regressive.

00:00:12.539 --> 00:00:19.119
They result in richer people paying a lower
percentage of their income to a VAT tax.

00:00:19.119 --> 00:00:23.619
And we talked in detail about how even when
you exempt some staples and put a higher VAT

00:00:23.619 --> 00:00:27.939
on luxury goods, it still ends up being regressive,
although sometimes less regressive than other

00:00:27.939 --> 00:00:29.029
times.

00:00:29.029 --> 00:00:33.870
This raised a new series of questions from
people in the audience about regressive versus

00:00:33.870 --> 00:00:35.160
progressive taxes.

00:00:35.160 --> 00:00:39.760
So what I want to do today is dig right in
and talk about this.

00:00:39.760 --> 00:00:42.250
There are three types of taxes.

00:00:42.250 --> 00:00:45.480
You can have a regressive, progressive, or
proportional tax.

00:00:45.480 --> 00:00:48.690
Now, what's important is that sometimes just
because something is called one thing doesn't

00:00:48.690 --> 00:00:52.040
mean it is that in reality, and we'll get
to that.

00:00:52.040 --> 00:00:57.360
A regressive tax has a greater impact on low
income individuals.

00:00:57.360 --> 00:01:02.910
A progressive tax has a greater impact on
higher income individuals.

00:01:02.910 --> 00:01:04.970
The more you earn, the more you pay.

00:01:04.970 --> 00:01:13.530
Uh, in terms of a, a rate and proportional
taxes apply, uh, to the, uh, to all incomes

00:01:13.530 --> 00:01:16.080
at the same rate, the same percentage.

00:01:16.080 --> 00:01:22.950
So some examples of regressive taxes include
the real estate property tax.

00:01:22.950 --> 00:01:27.180
If you look at the real estate property tax,
it tends to be the same regardless of the

00:01:27.180 --> 00:01:29.110
value of a property.

00:01:29.110 --> 00:01:34.210
If you consider the financial situation of
someone who has $100,000 property versus $1

00:01:34.210 --> 00:01:40.770
million property versus a $10 million property,
the fact that that tax is the, is a, a, a

00:01:40.770 --> 00:01:45.370
flat rate means that it will in practice be
regressive.

00:01:45.370 --> 00:01:48.550
Uh, as income goes up.

00:01:48.550 --> 00:01:55.220
An excise tax on consumables like gasoline
or airfare, taxes are effectively regressive.

00:01:55.220 --> 00:01:56.710
And we'll talk about that.

00:01:56.710 --> 00:02:00.520
Social security tax withholdings are regressive
because they are income caps.

00:02:00.520 --> 00:02:04.470
You pay up until you earn whatever amount
it is that the cap is.

00:02:04.470 --> 00:02:07.500
It's around 128,000 right now, I believe.

00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:11.989
And then once you get beyond that, you no
longer pay the social security tax, meaning

00:02:11.989 --> 00:02:18.520
that the more you earn, the lower your effective
social security tax rate is.

00:02:18.520 --> 00:02:21.170
Those are regressive taxes.

00:02:21.170 --> 00:02:25.049
Some progressive taxes include, for example,
the income tax.

00:02:25.049 --> 00:02:30.019
There are, uh, uh, uh, marginal tax rates
that increase as you earn more money.

00:02:30.019 --> 00:02:32.370
Now in practice, this is less clear.

00:02:32.370 --> 00:02:38.710
It very often the very, very, very rich, like
the 0.1% will pay a lower effective tax rate

00:02:38.710 --> 00:02:44.680
than the 1% because of a variety of different
loopholes and mechanisms that exist to reduce

00:02:44.680 --> 00:02:45.760
tax liability.

00:02:45.760 --> 00:02:50.579
But at least in theory, the income tax federally
is a progressive tax.

00:02:50.579 --> 00:02:57.470
The estate tax is a progressive tax, um, a
bunch of, uh, of a state.

00:02:57.470 --> 00:03:00.189
Um, inheritance is exempt.

00:03:00.189 --> 00:03:03.359
And then you start to see estate taxes come
in.

00:03:03.359 --> 00:03:05.740
And then lastly, there are some proportional
taxes.

00:03:05.740 --> 00:03:08.719
So example of a proportional tax would be
like a state income tax.

00:03:08.719 --> 00:03:16.709
In Massachusetts there is a 5.3%, maybe 5.35,
something like that, 5.3% state income tax.

00:03:16.709 --> 00:03:18.790
And that applies regardless of income.

00:03:18.790 --> 00:03:19.790
It is one rate.

00:03:19.790 --> 00:03:22.060
It is a proportional tax.

00:03:22.060 --> 00:03:26.560
Now the most important thing to consider,
a lot of this is pretty clear.

00:03:26.560 --> 00:03:32.730
The most important thing to think about is
how a tax that seems proportional can actually

00:03:32.730 --> 00:03:34.349
be regressive in practice.

00:03:34.349 --> 00:03:37.019
So think about a gasoline tax.

00:03:37.019 --> 00:03:39.900
Gasoline tax might mean we're making the numbers
up.

00:03:39.900 --> 00:03:44.069
Everybody pays 10 cents a gallon, poor people
pay 10 cents a gallon.

00:03:44.069 --> 00:03:46.109
Rich people pay 10 cents a gallon.

00:03:46.109 --> 00:03:48.859
This is effectively a regressive tax.

00:03:48.859 --> 00:03:51.489
It affects the poor more than the rich.

00:03:51.489 --> 00:03:53.279
Think about it this way.

00:03:53.279 --> 00:03:58.230
If everybody drives roughly 10,000 miles a
year, just because you have more money doesn't

00:03:58.230 --> 00:03:59.519
mean you necessarily drive more.

00:03:59.519 --> 00:04:03.489
In fact, sometimes rich people drive less
because they live closer to where they work.

00:04:03.489 --> 00:04:04.489
And there's other reasons.

00:04:04.489 --> 00:04:07.950
But imagine everybody drives roughly 10,000
miles a year.

00:04:07.950 --> 00:04:14.900
Um, the rich maybe have cars that, uh, uh,
use, consume more gas, but very often they

00:04:14.900 --> 00:04:15.900
don't.

00:04:15.900 --> 00:04:17.360
Uh, older cars tend to consume more gas.

00:04:17.360 --> 00:04:19.000
Poorer people have older cars.

00:04:19.000 --> 00:04:24.210
Let's again, imagine everybody drives 10,000
miles a year and has a car with roughly similar

00:04:24.210 --> 00:04:26.220
gas mileage.

00:04:26.220 --> 00:04:32.060
Everybody is paying, let's imagine 250 bucks
a year in gasoline taxes.

00:04:32.060 --> 00:04:38.920
Well, if you are in 25 grand a year, you've
paid 1% of your income to gasoline taxes.

00:04:38.920 --> 00:04:47.190
If you earn $250,000 a year, you've only paid
0.1% of a gasoline tax.

00:04:47.190 --> 00:04:53.820
So it's important to consider whether a tax
that sounds proportional is really proportional.

00:04:53.820 --> 00:04:59.960
There are a lot of proportional seeming taxes
that are actually regressive taxes.

00:04:59.960 --> 00:05:06.600
And again, anytime we talk about policy in
this way, we are not making any kind of assessment,

00:05:06.600 --> 00:05:12.160
at least in this segment about what is the
best or worst type of tax.

00:05:12.160 --> 00:05:15.960
We need to understand the policy types and
the tax types to then be able to ask the normal

00:05:15.960 --> 00:05:20.080
questions that you would when you want to
figure out how you want to tax, uh, a society

00:05:20.080 --> 00:05:21.080
and economy.

00:05:21.080 --> 00:05:27.150
What kind of behavior do we want to reward
or disincentivize in society and in the economy?

00:05:27.150 --> 00:05:32.040
When we think about fairness, what is fair
in terms of contribution to society?

00:05:32.040 --> 00:05:35.880
Should everyone pay the same percentage?

00:05:35.880 --> 00:05:40.190
Should people pay on the basis of their ability
to pay?

00:05:40.190 --> 00:05:46.790
Should those who have more greatly benefited
from the commons, uh, pay back into the system

00:05:46.790 --> 00:05:49.130
a larger share of their income?

00:05:49.130 --> 00:05:54.890
These often are economic questions combined
with philosophical questions, ideological

00:05:54.890 --> 00:05:57.670
questions, political questions, moral questions.

00:05:57.670 --> 00:06:01.010
In many ways, we're not doing any of that
today.

00:06:01.010 --> 00:06:06.650
All we're doing today is pointing out the
different of taxes that exist, how attacks

00:06:06.650 --> 00:06:10.180
can seem one thing and actually be the other.

00:06:10.180 --> 00:06:14.690
And then the last thing about this that I
think is important to mention when you have

00:06:14.690 --> 00:06:19.970
a tax, but then you have ways to circumvent
it.

00:06:19.970 --> 00:06:24.800
You have to consider that in your analysis
of whether it is actually progressive, regressive

00:06:24.800 --> 00:06:25.800
or proportional.

00:06:25.800 --> 00:06:28.890
The best example is we did this data last
year.

00:06:28.890 --> 00:06:32.600
Uh, we did this in 2019 based on 2018 data.

00:06:32.600 --> 00:06:36.510
You actually see that at the very top incomes.

00:06:36.510 --> 00:06:43.090
Even though on paper we have a progressive
tax code at the very, very high end, you see

00:06:43.090 --> 00:06:47.810
tax rates, effective tax rates often go down
because of the mechanisms and tools that are

00:06:47.810 --> 00:06:49.640
available to the very, very rich.

00:06:49.640 --> 00:06:51.990
So all important things to consider.

00:06:51.990 --> 00:06:56.150
Uh, and then only when we understand this,
can we actually start talking about what policies

00:06:56.150 --> 00:06:56.152
should be?

